|~ The command line: its topic, `::cli`, and the names it declares under
   the kinds of their subjects, `table` under `::vec` and the names of
   the streams under `::any`. Their primitives are plain functions over
   the served subject and the parts. ~|

:cli
  |~~ The command line runs a query on what a shell hands it and prints
      the answer: `qlang '/users | count' < data.json` in a script, and
      `qlang -i` a session of cells. It is the host a reader meets first,
      and the words it adds read and write the streams of the process.

      A query runs on what stdin holds. JSON is read as values, an object
      a map whose keys are keywords, `{"name": "Ann"}` arriving as
      `{:name "Ann"}`; any other text is one string, and `--raw` reads
      JSON text as a string too. A query that reads no subject,
      `qlang '::vec | doc'` or `qlang '[1 2] | sum'`, runs at once and
      reads no stdin. One that reads it with nothing piped, a terminal or
      `< /dev/null`, runs on the noun of the language, `::qlang`, so
      `qlang 'doc'` reads the first page; where a shell leaves stdin
      open, it waits for the end, as every reader of a pipe does.

      The answer is printed as the language prints it, whatever the input
      was: a map as `{:name "Ann" :tags #["a"]}`, a kind as `::map`. A
      string goes out as its text, so a query that ends in a conversion
      writes what the conversion made: `| json` the JSON text of the
      answer, `| table` a vector of maps drawn as a table, `| print` the
      literal of the answer, a string in its quotes. A failure prints as
      its alert, the error at rest `::Tag!{…}` with the subject where it
      arose cut to an excerpt, under the line
      `|~| raised, and no !| caught it` and above the prose of its
      refusal's page as line comments `|~| …`, which tells how to repair
      the call; `!| json` writes a failure as JSON. Every answer goes to
      stdout with the exit code 0, a failure among them: a query that does
      not parse answers `::quote/ParseError`, and an input that does not
      read as JSON answers as `parseJson` refuses its text, so calls run
      side by side never stop each other.

      An answer prints whole, and `--budget=N` or `| elide N` bounds it,
      what is left out an `::elision` marker whose `:read` continues the
      query to it. `@in`, `@out`, `@err` and `@tap` read and write the
      streams, `@tap` showing a value halfway through a query on stderr;
      a query that writes to stdout by `@out` holds the channel, and its
      answer is left unwritten. A value one query hands another over a
      pipe keeps its kinds as its literal, `print | @out` writing it and
      `@in | parse | open` reading it back.
      ::link~(::any/json | doc) ::link~(::vec/table | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
      ::link~(::any/@tap | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
  ~~|

:cli/declaresTable
  |~~ The command line declares `table` under vectors. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/table)

:cli/declaresTheStreams
  |~~ The command line declares the streams under any value. ~~|
  ::law~(::any | manifest | has ::any/@out)

:any/@in
  |~~ Answers the text stdin holds, read to its end whatever the subject,
      the text the command line also lifts into the subject; a terminal
      with nothing piped answers the empty string. It heads a query that
      reads the text as it came, ::snippet~(@in | lines | count) counting
      the lines piped in, and it takes no value.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@in/answer
  |~~ The text stdin held, the empty string when nothing was piped. ~~|
  ::string

:any/@out
  |~~ Writes a line to stdout and answers its subject as it came: it
      writes the subject when it is a string, `"hi" | @out`, or the text
      the call gives, computed against the subject, `42 | @out json`, the
      steps after still reading `42`. A query that writes by `@out`
      leaves its answer unwritten.
      ::snippet~([1 2] | @out json | count) writes `[1,2]`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@out/answer
  |~~ The subject as it came. ~~|
  ::any

:any/@out/text
  |~~ The text written, computed against the subject, null when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/@out/text/NotStringOrNullError
  |~~ The text `@out` writes is a string: a value of another kind is made
      text first, `@out json` or `@out print`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::null)

:any/@out/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `@out` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line; another
      value is written as the text the call computes from it,
      `42 | @out json` or `{:x 1} | @out print`.
  ~~|

:any/@out/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `@out` writes its subject, or the one text its call gives, `"hi" |
      @out`; texts are joined first, `@out (["a" "b"] | join "")`.
  ~~|

:any/@out/SubjectNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no line `@out` writes alone. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | @out !| type | eq ::any/@out/SubjectNotStringError)

:any/@out/text/NotStringOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no text to write. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | @out 5 !| type | eq ::any/@out/text/NotStringOrNullError)

:any/@out/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoTexts
  |~~ A call with two texts is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | @out "a" "b" !| type | eq ::any/@out/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/@err
  |~~ Writes a line to stderr, the channel of diagnostics, as `@out`
      writes to stdout, and answers its subject as it came; the answer of
      the query is written as ever.
      ::snippet~([1 2 3] | @err "counting" | count) writes `counting` to
      stderr and answers 3.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@err/answer
  |~~ The subject as it came. ~~|
  ::any

:any/@err/text
  |~~ The text written, computed against the subject, null when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/@err/text/NotStringOrNullError
  |~~ The text `@err` writes is a string: a value of another kind is made
      text first, `@err json` or `@err print`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::null)

:any/@err/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `@err` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line on stderr;
      another value is written as the text the call computes from it,
      `@err json` or `@err print`.
  ~~|

:any/@err/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `@err` writes its subject, or the one text its call gives, `"hi" |
      @err`; texts are joined first, `@err (["a" "b"] | join "")`.
  ~~|

:any/@err/SubjectNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no line `@err` writes alone. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | @err !| type | eq ::any/@err/SubjectNotStringError)

:any/@err/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoTexts
  |~~ A call with two texts is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | @err "a" "b" !| type | eq ::any/@err/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/@tap
  |~~ Writes `[tap <label>]` and the literal of its subject to stderr and
      answers the subject as it came, a look at a value halfway through a
      query:
      ::snippet~([{:on true} {:on false}] | @tap :before | filter ~(/on) | @tap :after | count)
      shows both vectors and answers 1.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@tap/answer
  |~~ The subject as it came. ~~|
  ::any

:any/@tap/label
  |~~ The label written before the value, a keyword. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/@tap/label/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The label of `@tap` is a keyword, `@tap :before`, written before
      the value as `[tap before]`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:any/@tap/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `@tap` writes its subject under one label its call gives, `5 | @tap
      :n`; a call with more labels is refused.
  ~~|

:any/@tap/label/NotKeywordError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no label. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | @tap "x" !| type | eq ::any/@tap/label/NotKeywordError)

:any/@tap/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoLabels
  |~~ A call with two labels is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | @tap :a :b !| type | eq ::any/@tap/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/table
  |~~ Answers a vector of maps drawn as a table of fixed-width columns, a
      text for a terminal. The columns are the keys in the order they
      first occur across the rows, and a key a row lacks draws an empty
      cell; a string cell prints bare, null as an empty cell, and every
      other value as its literal on one line. The empty vector draws as
      `(empty)`. A single map goes in a vector first, `[/] | table`, and
      the call takes no value.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/table/answer
  |~~ The rows drawn as text, a line each. ~~|
  ::string

:vec/table/SubjectNotVecError
  |~~ `table` draws a vector of maps; a single map goes in a vector
      first, `{:name "alice"} | [/] | table`.
  ~~|

:vec/table/RowNotMapError
  |~~ Every row of `table` is a map, its keys the columns; `:index`
      names the first that is not.
  ~~|

:vec/table/drawsEachRow
  |~~ Each row is drawn. ~~|
  ::law~([{:name "alice" :age 30} {:name "bob" :age 25}] | table | contains "alice")

:vec/table/drawsANestedValueAsItsLiteral
  |~~ A nested value is drawn as its literal. ~~|
  ::law~([{:at {:file "A.java" :line 12}}] | table | contains "{:file \"A.java\" :line 12}")

:vec/table/drawsNothingAsEmpty
  |~~ An empty vector draws as `(empty)`. ~~|
  ::law~([] | table | eq "(empty)")

:vec/table/drawsAMapInAVector
  |~~ A single map is drawn in a vector. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "alice"} | [/] | table | contains "alice")

:vec/table/SubjectNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A single map is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "alice"} | table !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::map])

:vec/table/SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no vector of rows. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | table !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::number])

:vec/table/RowNotMapError/refusesARowThatIsNoMap
  |~~ A row that is no map is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([42 "hello"] | table !| [type /index /actualType]
    | eq [::vec/table/RowNotMapError 0 ::number])
